<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :paint_charts %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:paint_charts) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Chart series') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every resolver takes an optional <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ el }</code>: the id
            or element whose nearest <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen</code> ancestor
            supplies the paint. Each returns a canonical
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Fill</code>; see
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %> for the shape.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series(i, count, { el })</code>: the fill for series
              <em>i</em> of <em>count</em> from the screen's chart-series ramp, which opens on the screen's ink.
              Series are spread across the legible front of the ramp; the span is the public
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--framework-chart-series-span</code> var, so a theme
              narrows it from CSS.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">ramp({ el })</code>: every chart-series slot as an
              array of Fills. CSS publishes how many there are in
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--framework-chart-series-count</code>, so a longer
              ramp reaches JavaScript without a framework release. Slots past the end of a shorter ramp come back
              empty.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Full-color screens draw their series from a color ramp: the ink, then seven hues in a fixed order, with
            steps picked for the light and dark ground separately. Grayscale and limited-palette screens keep the
            shade ladder, and a theme still overrides both with its own ramp.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// A single ramp step for series 1 of 4.
var s = TRMNLPaint.series(1, 4, { el: "my-chart" });

// Every ramp slot at once.
var fills = TRMNLPaint.ramp({ el: "my-chart" });', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Highcharts adapters') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Adapters shape a resolved <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Fill</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code> or
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TypeSpec</code> for one specific renderer. They copy
            resolved values into the renderer's native form; no adapter adds contrast heuristics, thresholds or
            substitute design rules.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">toHighcharts(fill)</code>: a solid Fill returns a
              flat color string; a tile Fill returns a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ pattern: { image, width, height, backgroundColor } }</code>
              object for the Highcharts pattern-fill module. The field color is composited into the pattern image
              beneath the ink paths, matching CSS layer order, so the tile is self-contained. An empty Fill returns
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">null</code>.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The axis and label adapters live with their resolvers:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">toHighchartsAxis()</code> on
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_borders) %>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">toHighchartsText()</code> on
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_typography) %>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code> (see the next section) is the
            Highcharts composition layer over TRMNLPaint: its
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">grid()</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">axisLine()</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">textStyle()</code> methods select the documented chart
            roles and delegate resolution and conversion here. Adapters for other libraries (a Chart.js
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">CanvasPattern</code>, a D3
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">&lt;pattern&gt;</code>) are a natural sibling shape.
            Until one ships, resolve a Fill and translate it yourself; the Fill already carries everything a library
            needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// Highcharts: flat color on solid panels, a self-contained pattern tile
// on 1- and 2-bit screens.
var hc = TRMNLPaint.toHighcharts(TRMNLPaint.series(0, 4, { el: "my-chart" }));

// Any other library: resolve a Fill and translate it however you like.
var fill = TRMNLPaint.bg("gray-30", { el: "my-chart" });
var css = fill.image
  ? fill.image + " repeat"   // dither tile
  : fill.color;              // solid', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The TRMNLCharts API') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code> ships in the same
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.js</code> runtime as
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> and composes Highcharts options out
            of it. It resolves no paint of its own: every value it returns comes from a TRMNLPaint resolver and
            adapter. <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %> puts it to work in line, bar, and gauge charts.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLMaps</code> does the same for MapLibre GL JS,
            composing map styles out of the map slots: see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_maps) %>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every method takes the same optional <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ el }</code> as
            the resolvers: the chart container id or element whose nearest
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen</code> supplies the paint. Omit it on a
            single-screen plugin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Resolving chart paint') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">paint(token, { el })</code>: one palette token as a
                Highcharts fill, flat color in solid modes and a pattern object in dither modes.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series(i, count, { el })</code>: the Highcharts fill
                for series <em>i</em> of <em>count</em> from the screen's chart-series ramp.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applySwatches({ el })</code>: paints every element
                under the screen carrying <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">data-chart-series="i"</code>
                from that same ramp, so legend markers and series stay in lockstep. An optional
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">data-chart-series-count</code> sets the series total;
                it defaults to the number of tagged elements. Call it every time the chart builds.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Building the options') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">options({ el })</code>: the recommended Highcharts
                options for the TRMNL aesthetic, with a transparent background, no animation, no chrome, and
                framework-resolved axes and type.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">merge(base, overrides)</code>: a deep merge of two
                plain objects, where arrays and scalars replace. Layer your chart's own config over
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">options()</code> with it.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">grid({ el, dir })</code>: the grid-line options
                (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gridLineColor</code>,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gridLineWidth</code>,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">gridLineDashStyle</code>) from the muted themed
                hairline, border step 65. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">dir: 'h'</code> (the
                default) is the horizontal yAxis grid,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'v'</code> the vertical xAxis grid.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">axisLine({ el })</code>: the
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">lineColor</code> and
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">tickColor</code> options from the black border
                rail, which axis and ticks share.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">textStyle(role, { el })</code>: a Highcharts text
                style for one framework typography role. It carries the resolved font properties and an opaque ink
                color, and sets <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">textOutline: 'none'</code> to kill
                the default white halo on data labels. Pass
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ stroke: '&lt;token&gt;' }</code> for an
                intentional outline.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Keeping the chart current') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch(el, buildFn)</code>: builds the chart now and
                again whenever the device, scale, mode, dark-mode or theme classes change on the screen or on a
                wrapper above it.
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">buildFn</code> creates and returns the chart
                instance; the previous one is destroyed before each rebuild. Returns a stop function.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
              Highcharts numbers do not read CSS, so resolve heights, spacing, and offsets with
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code> inside the build function. See
              <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: 'var el = "my-chart";

// watch() rebuilds on every device, mode, dark-mode and theme change.
TRMNLCharts.watch(el, function () {
  var chart = Highcharts.chart(el, TRMNLCharts.merge(TRMNLCharts.options({ el: el }), {
    chart: { type: "column", height: TRMNLPaint.px(180, { el: el }) },
    xAxis: { categories: ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed"] },
    series: [
      { name: "Sent", data: [3, 5, 8], color: TRMNLCharts.series(0, 2, { el: el }) },
      { name: "Read", data: [2, 4, 6], color: TRMNLCharts.series(1, 2, { el: el }) }
    ]
  }));
  // Legend markers marked data-chart-series="0" / "1" pick up the same ramp.
  TRMNLCharts.applySwatches({ el: el });
  return chart;
});', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Live example') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The bar rows below are painted with
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.series()</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.apply()</code>, wrapped in
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.watch()</code>, with no charting library
            involved. Change the device mode or Style in the screen picker and the bars repaint from the live
            cascade: flat colors on solid panels, dither tiles on 1- and 2-bit screens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_example_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_example_inner_classes %>">
            <div class="<%= framework_example_wrapper_classes %>">
              <div class="screen">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <div class="layout layout--col gap--space-between">
                    <div class="grid">
                      <div class="item col--span-3">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <span class="value value--large value--tnums" data-fit-value="true">$48.6k</span>
                          <span class="label">Paper Sales</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item col--span-3">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <span class="value value--large value--tnums" data-fit-value="true">6</span>
                          <span class="label">Branches</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="divider"></div>

                    <div id="paint-strip" class="flex flex--col gap--small w--full"></div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="title_bar">
                    <img class="image image--adaptive" src="/images/plugins/trmnl--render.svg" alt="TRMNL Logo">
                    <span class="title">Paint API</span>
                    <span class="instance">TRMNLPaint.apply</span>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <script type="text/javascript">
                  // Docs example iframes load plugins.js AFTER inline scripts, so wait
                  // for TRMNLPaint before painting.
                  function whenReady(cb) {
                    var tries = 0;
                    (function attempt() {
                      if (window.TRMNLPaint) return cb();
                      if (++tries > 200) return;
                      setTimeout(attempt, 50);
                    })();
                  }

                  whenReady(function () {
                    var el = "paint-strip";
                    var DATA = [["Scranton", 12.4], ["Stamford", 9.8], ["Nashua", 8.6],
                                ["Utica", 7.4], ["Albany", 6.2], ["Buffalo", 4.2]];
                    // watch() repaints whenever the screen device/scale/mode/dark/theme classes change,
                    // re-reading paint from the live cascade each time.
                    TRMNLPaint.watch(el, function () {
                      var box = document.getElementById(el);
                      if (!box) return;
                      box.innerHTML = "";
                      DATA.forEach(function (d, i) {
                        var row = document.createElement("div");
                        row.className = "flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--small";
                        var cap = document.createElement("span");
                        cap.className = "label label--small";
                        cap.style.cssText = "width:88px;flex:none;";
                        cap.textContent = d[0];
                        var rail = document.createElement("div");
                        rail.style.cssText = "flex:1;height:16px;";
                        var bar = document.createElement("div");
                        bar.style.cssText = "height:100%;border-radius:4px;width:" + (d[1] / DATA[0][1] * 100) + "%;";
                        // series(i, n) resolves the fill for series i from the screen's
                        // chart ramp; apply() composites field + tile like the CSS pipeline.
                        TRMNLPaint.apply(bar, TRMNLPaint.series(i, DATA.length, { el: el }));
                        rail.appendChild(bar);
                        var val = document.createElement("span");
                        val.className = "label label--small";
                        val.style.cssText = "width:48px;flex:none;text-align:right;";
                        val.textContent = "$" + d[1].toFixed(1) + "k";
                        row.appendChild(cap);
                        row.appendChild(rail);
                        row.appendChild(val);
                        box.appendChild(row);
                      });
                    });
                  });
                </script>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '<!-- an empty, ID\'d container to paint into -->
<div id="paint-strip" class="flex flex--col gap--small w--full"></div>

<script type="text/javascript">
  // plugins.js bundles TRMNLPaint; wait for it before painting.
  function whenReady(cb) {
    var tries = 0;
    (function attempt() {
      if (window.TRMNLPaint) return cb();
      if (++tries > 200) return;
      setTimeout(attempt, 50);
    })();
  }

  whenReady(function () {
    var el = "paint-strip";
    var DATA = [["Scranton", 12.4], ["Stamford", 9.8], ["Nashua", 8.6],
                ["Utica", 7.4], ["Albany", 6.2], ["Buffalo", 4.2]];
    // watch() repaints whenever the screen device/scale/mode/dark/theme classes change,
    // re-reading paint from the live cascade each time.
    TRMNLPaint.watch(el, function () {
      var box = document.getElementById(el);
      if (!box) return;
      box.innerHTML = "";
      DATA.forEach(function (d, i) {
        var row = document.createElement("div");
        row.className = "flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--small";
        var cap = document.createElement("span");
        cap.className = "label label--small";
        cap.style.cssText = "width:88px;flex:none;";
        cap.textContent = d[0];
        var rail = document.createElement("div");
        rail.style.cssText = "flex:1;height:16px;";
        var bar = document.createElement("div");
        bar.style.cssText = "height:100%;border-radius:4px;width:" + (d[1] / DATA[0][1] * 100) + "%;";
        // series(i, n) resolves the fill for series i from the screen\'s chart
        // ramp; apply() composites field + tile like the CSS pipeline.
        TRMNLPaint.apply(bar, TRMNLPaint.series(i, DATA.length, { el: el }));
        rail.appendChild(bar);
        row.appendChild(cap);
        row.appendChild(rail);
        box.appendChild(row);
      });
    });
  });
</script>', language: 'html') %>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
